## What was checked

The component and pattern indexes were read on **12 November 2026**. The Publishing frontend-template index was also checked. The inventory records direct entries, including the Home Office's named query routes; it does not count every descendant example as a separate component. Royal Navy contributes 37 distinct titles from its current Storybook's `Components/` namespace, deduplicating documentation and variant stories. Other Storybook namespaces are outside that catalogue count. The existing Publishing inventory includes 42 pages across nested examples and variations, while this top-level review records 26 direct entries. Those counts have different scopes.

Specific rules were checked against their owning guidance where they affect a decision: GOV.UK slider questions, numeric entry, grouping, choice, summary and error components, NHS cards/images, Home Office long-list guidance, W3C slider/dragging requirements, the MOJ notification badge and status model, DWP's retired key details bar and the Home Office time-display component. Entries marked **not selected** are recorded for completeness. They are not adopted and are not a completed detail-page assessment.

GOV.UK brand foundations remain covered by the [brand guidance inventory](../reference/brand-guidance.json). Its detailed chapter review keeps its own date. This review rechecked the current brand landing structure. Supplied Civica artwork, Roboto typography and the prohibition on official government branding still apply.

## The slider finding

**There is a current official UK range-slider component:** the [Royal Navy Range Slider](https://storybook.design-system.navy.digital.mod.uk/?path=/docs/components-range-slider--docs). The first eight catalogues did not list one; widening the check found this ninth source. The first finding was too narrow to answer a question about all UK systems. The current Storybook lists ten slider stories, including multiple handles, steps, labels, formatting, reversed scales, thresholds and a disabled state.

The owning repository's [4.64.2 release](https://github.com/Royal-Navy/design-system/releases/tag/4.64.2), published on 11 November 2026, contains the component. The live Storybook index does not declare its deployed package version, so a release and a Storybook are separate pieces of evidence. The [Design guidance page](https://design-system.navy.digital.mod.uk/guidance/design) explicitly calls itself legacy; its Axure library version 3.2 is not the React package version. Neither a current release nor a working story establishes accessibility certification.

GOV.UK [Question pages](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/patterns/question-pages/#using-range-sliders) advises against using sliders for questions and requires an alternative to dragging when they are used. Home Assistant still has legitimate continuous-adjustment tasks. The [Civica slider component](../components/sliders.html) compares the Royal Navy's bounded scale, displayed value, steps and disabled-state anatomy with the local household requirements. Its original implementation uses a native range with precise text entry and visible step buttons, all editing one draft. It follows the [W3C slider pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/slider/) and separates requested values from reported state.

The Royal Navy React package, branding and threshold meanings are not imported. The reference does not replace native Home Assistant sliders. The [continuous-controls pattern](../home-assistant/continuous-controls.html) remains responsible for integration ranges, commits, remote updates and failures. The [USWDS slider](https://designsystem.digital.gov/components/range-slider/) remains an explicitly United States comparator; it is no longer the only published comparator recorded here.

